&lt;h3 id="-overview"&gt;
 ⭐ Overview
 &lt;a class="anchor" href="#-overview"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The five key types of meaning in language: &lt;strong&gt;lexical&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;phrasal (sentential) semantic&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;grammatical&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;discourse-related&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;pragmatic&lt;/strong&gt;—and how they interact with syntax .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Syntax–semantics mismatches&lt;/strong&gt;: When universal meanings (e.g., ongoing/habitual events) are encoded by different forms across languages (e.g., English past progressive vs. Spanish Imperfect) .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Poverty of the Stimulus (POS) in semantics&lt;/strong&gt;: How L2 learners acquire meanings not attested in input (e.g., missing interpretations of impersonal &lt;em&gt;se&lt;/em&gt; in Spanish preterit) .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Special learning challenges: &lt;strong&gt;novel constructions&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., French discontinuous interrogatives) and &lt;strong&gt;morphologically unmarked meanings&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., English articles for Russian/Korean learners) .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
