 ⭐ Overview
 &lt;a class="anchor" href="#-overview"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The two key external interfaces:
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Syntax–Discourse Interface&lt;/strong&gt;: How syntax encodes discourse information (e.g., Topic/Focus via word order, clitics) .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Semantics–Pragmatics Interface&lt;/strong&gt;: How context and universal pragmatic principles (e.g., Gricean maxims) adjust literal meaning (e.g., scalar implicatures) .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Why interface properties are often harder to acquire: They require integrating linguistic (syntax/semantics) and extralinguistic (context/culture) information—taxing working memory .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Key phenomena: &lt;strong&gt;Topic/Focus marking&lt;/strong&gt; (clitic doubling, word order), &lt;strong&gt;pronoun reference&lt;/strong&gt; (null/overt pronoun division of labor), &lt;strong&gt;intonation as discourse cue&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;scalar implicatures&lt;/strong&gt; .&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
